          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Typically five to twelve hours of realistic intermittent pumping on a single group 27 deep-cycle battery, though the honest answer depends entirely on how often the pump cycles. In light seepage a battery may last a day or more; during heavy sustained inflow the same battery might manage three or four hours. Dual-battery systems roughly double it." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a portable generator run a sump pump?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Comfortably. A typical one-third to one-half horsepower sump pump draws roughly 800 to 1,050 running watts with a starting surge of two to three times that, so a 2,000 watt generator handles it with room to spare and a 3,000 to 4,000 watt machine covers the pump plus a fridge and lighting. Sizing is covered in detail in our guide to what size generator runs a sump pump." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do you need both a battery backup and a generator?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "In a basement where flooding is a genuine risk, yes, and the two work well together. The battery covers the first hours automatically — including the three in the morning start of an outage, and any outage while you are away — and the generator takes over for a multi-day event once someone is there to run it. Many owners use the generator to recharge the backup battery as well." },
        },
